tel 1.0

base mercator
import soundness

info   `Identity`, `Functor` and `Monad` typeclasses derived for any type with `apply`, `map` and `flatMap`
detail
    _Mercator_ abstracts over containers — `Option`, `Try`, the standard collections, `Either`,
    a `parasite` `Task` — whose `map` and `flatMap` share names but no common type, so that an
    operation can be written once over all of them. Its three typeclasses form a ladder:
    `Identity` lifts a value into the container (`point`), `Functor` adds mapping, and `Monad`
    adds `bind`, which is `flatMap` under a name the container does not already have.

    Instances need no writing. Each companion holds an `inline given` macro which constructs the
    instance from the methods the type already has: `Identity` from the companion's single
    `apply` taking one element, `Functor` from the type's single `map`, and `Monad` from its
    single `flatMap`. A container written with none of this in mind takes part anyway, and one
    whose methods are ambiguous or missing is a compile error naming the problem.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import scala.util.{Try, Success}

    val numbers: scala.collection.immutable.List[Try[Int]] =
      scala.collection.immutable.List(Try(1), Try(2), Try(3))

    numbers.sequence   // Success(List(1, 2, 3))
    ```

    Two operations follow at once, and are the reason to have the typeclasses: `sequence`
    turns a collection of containers inside out, and `traverse` maps a fallible function over a
    collection and gathers the results into one container — "parse all of these, and fail if
    any fails". Both work over the standard library's `Iterable` collections; Soundness's own
    opaque `List`, `Set` and `Vector` are not `Iterable`s and do not take part.

    The library has a single source set, `mercator.core`. `Cofunctor`, `Functor`, `Identity`,
    `Monad`, `sequence` and `traverse` are exported to the `soundness` umbrella. The extension
    methods `map` and `bind` are **not**: those names belong to the collection methods of
    `murmuration` and `rudiments` under `import soundness.*`, so mercator's are reached with
    `import mercator.bind` or `import mercator.map` (or `import mercator.*`), and code importing
    both `mercator.*` and `rudiments.*` may find `bind` on `Text` ambiguous.

refer mercator.Monad
refer mercator.Functor
refer mercator.Identity
refer mercator.sequence
refer mercator.traverse

entry .Identity   a typeclass lifting a single value into a container: `point`
  detail
      `Identity[F]` has one method, `#point`, which makes an `F[value]` holding just `value`:
      `Some(value)`, `List(value)`, `Success(value)`, `Right(value)`. It is the base of
      `Functor` and `Monad`, whose derivations look for one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      summon[Identity[Option]].point(1)          // Some(1)
      summon[Identity[scala.util.Try]].point(1)  // Success(1)
      ```

      For any type constructor but `Either`, the instance is derived by `.ident` from the
      companion's `apply`.
  refer mercator.Functor
  entry .either   `Identity` for `Either[?, value]`, whose `point` is `Right(value)`
      In implicit scope. `Either`'s companion has no `apply`, so the derived `.ident` cannot
      serve it; this instance gives the right-biased point.
  entry .ident   derives `Identity[F]` for any type constructor whose companion has one suitable `apply`
      An `inline given` macro, in implicit scope. It looks in the companion of `F` for `apply`
      methods with a single type parameter taking one value of that type — directly, by name,
      or as a variadic parameter, as `List.apply(elements*)` does — and builds `#point` as a
      call to it. No candidate is a compile error, _the companion object `Play` has no candidate
      apply methods_ (SN-569), and more than one is another, _the companion object `Play` has
      more than one candidate apply method_ (SN-659).
  entry #point   lifts a single value into the container

entry .Functor   a typeclass mapping a function over a container's contents, with `point` from `Identity`
  detail
      `Functor[F]` provides `#apply`, which maps a function over an `F[value]` to give an
      `F[value2]`, the `#map` extension which calls it, and `#point`, which lifts a value. It is
      derived, by `.general`, for any type with a single `map` method and an `Identity`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val functor = summon[Functor[Option]]
      functor.map(Some(1))(_ + 1)   // Some(2)
      ```

      A `Functor` is `Findable`.
  refer mercator.Identity
  refer mercator.Monad
  entry .general   derives `Functor[F]` for any type with a single `map` method and an `Identity[F]`
      An `inline given` macro, in implicit scope. `#apply` calls the type's own `map`, and
      `#point` the `Identity` it finds. A missing `Identity` is a compile error, _could not find
      Identity value for `Play`_ (SN-970); a type with no `map` is another, _the type `Play` has
      no map methods_ (SN-899), and one with several overloads of `map` a third, _the type
      `Play` has more than one possible map method_ (SN-380).
  entry #point   lifts a single value into the container
  entry #apply   maps a function over a container's contents: `functor(value)(lambda)`
  entry #map   the extension form of `#apply`, on any value of the container type
      Usable on an `F[value]` wherever this `Functor[F]` is in lexical scope as a value, as in
      `functor.map(Some(1))(_ + 1)`; the package-level `map` extension finds the `Functor` by
      contextual search instead.

entry .Monad   a typeclass sequencing computations in a container: `bind`, with `map` and `point`
  detail
      `Monad[F]` extends `Functor[F]` with `#bind`, which applies a function returning a
      container to a container's contents and flattens the result: `flatMap`, under a name
      which does not collide with the container's own method. It is derived, by `.monad`, for
      any type with a single `flatMap` method and a `Functor`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val monad = summon[Monad[Option]]
      monad.bind(Some(1)) { value => Some(value + 1) }   // Some(2)
      ```

      Instances which cannot be derived are written by hand: `parasite`'s `Task` has a
      `Monad[Task]` in its companion, needing a `Monitor` and a probate in scope. A `Monad` is
      `Findable`. `sequence` and `traverse` are the operations every `Monad` makes available
      over a collection.
  refer mercator.Functor
  refer mercator.sequence
  refer mercator.traverse
  refer mercator.bind
  entry .monad   derives `Monad[F]` for any type with a single `flatMap` method and a `Functor[F]`
      An `inline given` macro, in implicit scope. `#bind` calls the type's own `flatMap`, and
      `point` and mapping come from the `Functor` it finds. A missing `Functor` is a compile
      error, _could not find Functor value for `Play`_ (SN-585); a type with no `flatMap` is
      another, _the type `Play` has no flatMap methods_ (SN-356), and one with several overloads
      of `flatMap` a third, _the type `Play` has more than one possible flatMap method_ (SN-538).
  entry #bind   applies a container-returning function to a container's contents, and flattens
      `monad.bind(value)(lambda)`: `flatMap` by another name.
  entry #flatMap   the extension form of `#bind`, so that `for`-comprehensions work through a `Monad`
      Usable on an `F[value]` wherever this `Monad[F]` is in lexical scope as a value.

entry .Cofunctor   a typeclass mapping a function backwards into a contravariant container: `contramap`
  detail
      `Cofunctor[F]` is the dual of `Functor` for a contravariant `F[-value]`, such as a
      consumer, an encoder or an ordering: given a function `value2 => value`, it turns an
      `F[value]` into an `F[value2]` by adapting its input. Unlike the other three typeclasses,
      it has no derivation: an instance is written by hand.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      trait Sink[-value]:
        def accept(value: value): Unit

      given Cofunctor[Sink]:
        def apply[value, value2](sink: Sink[value])(lambda: value2 => value): Sink[value2] =
          value2 => sink.accept(lambda(value2))
      ```
  refer mercator.Functor
  entry #apply   adapts a contravariant container to accept another type: `cofunctor(value)(lambda)`
  entry #contramap   the extension form of `#apply`, on any value of the contravariant type

entry .map   maps a function over any container with a `Functor`, as an extension method
    `value.map(lambda)` for any `F[value]` with a `Functor[F]` in scope. It is not exported to
    the `soundness` umbrella, where `map` is the collection method of `murmuration`; import it
    as `import mercator.map`. Since most containers have a `map` method of their own, and a
    method always beats an extension, this matters only to generic code over an abstract `F`:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import mercator.map

    def increment[F[_]: Functor](numbers: F[Int]): F[Int] = numbers.map(_ + 1)
    ```

entry .bind   monadic bind over any container with a `Monad`, and character-wise bind over `Text`
    Two extension methods share the name. For any `F[value]` with a `Monad[F]` in scope,
    `value.bind(lambda)` is `flatMap`. On `Text`, `text.bind(lambda)` applies a `Char => Text`
    function to every character and joins the results.

    Neither is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, where `bind` is the collection method of
    `rudiments`; import it as `import mercator.bind`. A file importing both `mercator.*` and
    `rudiments.*` will find `bind` ambiguous on `Text`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import mercator.bind

    def twice[F[_]: Monad](value: F[Int], step: Int => F[Int]): F[Int] =
      value.bind(step).bind(step)

    t"abc".bind { char => t"$char$char" }   // t"aabbcc"
    ```

entry .sequence   turns a collection of containers inside out: `List[Try[Int]]` to `Try[List[Int]]`
    An extension on any collection of `F[element]`, with a `Monad[F]` in scope, which combines
    the containers in order and rebuilds a collection of the same kind inside one container: `Success` of every value if all succeed, the first `Failure`
    otherwise; for `Option`, `None` if any is `None`; for collections, every combination.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import scala.util.Try

    List(Try(1), Try(2), Try(3)).sequence   // Success(List(1, 2, 3))
    ```

    The collection is rebuilt through its `Reshapable` instance, so a `List` sequences to a
    `List` and a `Set` to a `Set`.

entry .traverse   maps a container-returning function over a collection and gathers the results in one container
    `elements.traverse(lambda)` is `elements.map(lambda).sequence` in a single pass: an
    extension on any collection, for a function `element => F[element2]` with
    a `Monad[F]` in scope. It is the shape of "parse all of these, and fail if any fails".

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    def parse(text: String): Option[Int] = text.toIntOption

    List("1", "2", "3").traverse(parse)   // Some(List(1, 2, 3))
    List("1", "x").traverse(parse)        // None
    ```

    As with `sequence`, the result has the collection's own shape.

entry .internal   the macros which derive `Identity`, `Functor` and `Monad` instances
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the givens `Identity.ident`, `Functor.general` and `Monad.monad` splice
      these.
  entry .point   derives an `Identity` from a companion's `apply`
  entry .functor   derives a `Functor` from a type's `map`
  entry .monad   derives a `Monad` from a type's `flatMap`
